    ('e4d98868-169b-40c4-bac5-569a5a5c69bb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The fort at Watercrook was erected around AD 90, originally in timber but then rebuilt in stone around the year 130 during the reign of Hadrian.', 'Alavana is a name tentatively, and probably wrongly, given to the ruins of a Roman fort at Watercrook in Cumbria, raised in timber around AD 90 and rebuilt in stone around 130 under Hadrian.', 'teaser-card-s031:e4d98868-169b-40c4-bac5-569a5a5c69bb:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '961aab050ee44535565be74061ce03763f020dbeec7c6f27eb8acf2f3bd22c25|3cf5f682789ae6064552c3ac634c719a0704a49124f9e9d65d4c39c733306f7e|3cf5f682789ae6064552c3ac634c719a0704a49124f9e9d65d4c39c733306f7e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-027", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Alavana is a name tentatively, and probably wrongly, given to the ruins of a Roman fort at Watercrook in Cumbria, raised in timber around AD 90 and rebuilt in stone around 130 under Hadrian."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-027 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Alavana is a name applied to the ruins of a Roman fort [S1]; the name was applied tentatively and probably wrongly (hedge kept) [S1]; the fort is at Watercrook in Cumbria [S1]; the fort was raised in timber around AD 90 [S2]; it was rebuilt in stone around 130 [S2]; the stone rebuilding was under Hadrian [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-080 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Alavana is the ruins of a Roman fort (the kind of place the text is about) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alavana]; The name Alavana is only tentatively, and probably wrongly, given to the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alavana]; The fort is at Watercrook [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alavana]; Watercrook is south of Kendal in Cumbria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alavana]; The fort was raised in timber around AD 90 [SUPPORTED: https://www.roman-britain.co.uk/places/watercrook-fort/]; The fort was rebuilt in stone around AD 130 under Hadrian [SUPPORTED: https://www.roman-britain.co.uk/places/watercrook-fort/]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e50caf34-c829-4f2c-b87c-3311958ef369'::uuid, 'card_description', 'During the events of the Roman Civil War, "Segeda II" was ruined, and after 49 BC it was abandoned permanently.', 'In 153 BC, the Periochae say, the Roman Senate moved the first day of the consular year to 1 January so that a consul could attack Segeda, a Celtiberian town of the Belli.', 'teaser-card-s031:e50caf34-c829-4f2c-b87c-3311958ef369:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 171 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '268d87f2135aee00c64f276b41706f51f31c6611512b472b54d63efa2d442e4d|9841bf0fc697d9405e1654030a650e46e89666d1fd8b388cd295a9250c101325|9841bf0fc697d9405e1654030a650e46e89666d1fd8b388cd295a9250c101325', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-027", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 153 BC, the Periochae say, the Roman Senate moved the first day of the consular year to 1 January so that a consul could attack Segeda, a Celtiberian town of the Belli."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-027 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "in 153 BC the Roman Senate changed the first day of the consular year to 1 January [S3]; the source for this is the Periochae [S3]; the change was made so that a consul could attack Segeda [S3]; Segeda was a Celtiberian town [S2]; its inhabitants were the Belli [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-081 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Segeda is an ancient settlement (a town) in Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segeda]; Segeda was a Celtiberian town of the Belli [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segeda]; The Periochae report the events of 153 BC concerning Segeda [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segeda]; In 153 BC the Roman Senate moved the first day of the consular year to 1 January [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segeda]; The change was made so that a consul could attack Segeda [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segeda]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e55a8540-286d-4182-8370-3ffc69280f6d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Small Aten Temple is a temple to the Aten located in the ancient Egyptian city of Amarna.', 'In central Amarna stood the Small Aten Temple, first known as the Mansion of the Aten and probably built before the Great Temple. Its only contemporary depiction is in the tomb of Tutu.', 'teaser-card-s031:e55a8540-286d-4182-8370-3ffc69280f6d:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'c7f2c3733596d86ad956576fc872584b76530e2e5f995f996b3f1d17da34a840|b172d98726eb7523e54c47d49e682f1657d007514455ad7a0874dc5cb00b5156|b172d98726eb7523e54c47d49e682f1657d007514455ad7a0874dc5cb00b5156', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-027", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In central Amarna stood the Small Aten Temple, first known as the Mansion of the Aten and probably built before the Great Temple. Its only contemporary depiction is in the tomb of Tutu."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-027 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Small Aten Temple was in the central part of Amarna [S1, S3]; it was first known as the Mansion of the Aten [S4]; it was probably built before the Great Temple (hedge kept) [S4]; its only contemporary depiction is in the tomb of Tutu [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-081 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Small Aten Temple was a temple in the ancient Egyptian city of Amarna [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Aten_Temple]; It stood in central Amarna [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Aten_Temple]; It was first known as the Mansion of the Aten [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Aten_Temple]; It was probably built before the Great Temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Aten_Temple]; Its only contemporary depiction is in the tomb of Tutu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Aten_Temple]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e59377f7-18eb-459e-adc4-b9c40274ddfa'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Stone chullpa tombs, walls, houses, and squares at 2,800 metres elevation in Carabaya Province. A highland community built in stone, set high on the Andean plateau.', 'At about 2,800 m in the Puno Region, Chichakuri holds stone tombs called chullpa among its walls, houses and squares, a place declared a National Cultural Heritage on May 16, 2003.', 'teaser-card-s031:e59377f7-18eb-459e-adc4-b9c40274ddfa:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 180 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '656ec66a49ed2c12201b2f0a992609d9a160901b246c7057d9d1ffaaf2d803f3|518e29237ef15b5cf59f2329f99588635a0ded9ee45e9be878f81e7b095b37fe|518e29237ef15b5cf59f2329f99588635a0ded9ee45e9be878f81e7b095b37fe', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-027", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At about 2,800 m in the Puno Region, Chichakuri holds stone tombs called chullpa among its walls, houses and squares, a place declared a National Cultural Heritage on May 16, 2003."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-027 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chichakuri is at about 2,800 m [S3]; it is in the Puno Region [S3]; it holds stone tombs called chullpa [S1]; it also has walls, houses and squares [S1]; it was declared a National Cultural Heritage on May 16, 2003 [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-081 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chichakuri is an archaeological site in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichakuri]; Chichakuri holds stone tombs called chullpa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichakuri]; Chichakuri also has walls, houses and squares [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichakuri]; Chichakuri lies in the Puno Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichakuri]; Chichakuri lies at about 2,800 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichakuri]; The place was declared a National Cultural Heritage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichakuri]; The declaration was on May 16, 2003 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichakuri]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e6b17dcc-68fc-40ff-b2ab-b541655e14e2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Capital of Urartu''s Transcaucasian provinces in the 7th century BC. Its ground floor was entirely filled with storage rooms for grain and oil reserves.', 'Near Yerevan, Rusa II built Teishebaini in the mid-7th century BC to guard the eastern borders of Urartu: towered walls, massive gates and a stone palace of a hundred and twenty rooms.', 'teaser-card-s031:e6b17dcc-68fc-40ff-b2ab-b541655e14e2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'd35073e1b31112a3569c95767fe4d301c6da2e2c86b013a680de36c69ae9ed23|b3483f2fbe744768c1c1ae4315eee3defcb15b014ccea8de8fe1b5717526246b|b3483f2fbe744768c1c1ae4315eee3defcb15b014ccea8de8fe1b5717526246b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-027", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Yerevan, Rusa II built Teishebaini in the mid-7th century BC to guard the eastern borders of Urartu: towered walls, massive gates and a stone palace of a hundred and twenty rooms."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-027 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Teishebaini is near Yerevan [S2]; Rusa II built Teishebaini [S4]; it was built in the mid-7th century BC [S4]; it was built to guard the eastern borders of Urartu [S4]; it had towered walls [S3]; it had massive gates [S3]; it had a palace of stone [S6]; the palace had a hundred and twenty rooms [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-081 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Teishebaini was a fortress (a fortified city and governmental centre) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; Teishebaini is near Yerevan, in Armenia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; Rusa II built Teishebaini [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; It was built in the mid-7th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; It was built to guard the eastern borders of Urartu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; It had towered walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; It had massive gates [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; It had a palace [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; The palace had a hundred and twenty rooms [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]; The palace was made of stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teishebaini]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e6b248f2-394d-44ea-8283-e9fad9ae5449'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A bridge of the Roman aqueduct to Nimes, on the same system as the famous Pont du Gard. Its three segmental arches total 17 metres, with two now buried underground.', 'The Pont de Bornègre carried the Roman aqueduct to Nîmes over a torrent. Sediment buries two of its three arches up to their vaults; once the aqueduct decayed, people crossed it on foot.', 'teaser-card-s031:e6b248f2-394d-44ea-8283-e9fad9ae5449:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '59cbde2d18e6dd43c77199bdb9166e576f188ed4e08bac001a3f6c991ca4ec59|a8a9b55cbe06461c684998f41fca6922240ae8079b5bd23ce2e4709372ffdf35|a8a9b55cbe06461c684998f41fca6922240ae8079b5bd23ce2e4709372ffdf35', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Pont de Bornègre carried the Roman aqueduct to Nîmes over a torrent. Sediment buries two of its three arches up to their vaults; once the aqueduct decayed, people crossed it on foot."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Pont de Bornègre carried the Roman aqueduct to Nîmes [S1]; it bridged a torrent [S3]; it has three arches [S4]; sediment buries two of its three arches [S5]; the arches are buried up to the vaults (springing line) [S5]; once the aqueduct fell into disrepair, the bridge was used by people crossing on foot [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-081 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Pont de Bornègre is an ancient bridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_de_Born%C3%A8gre]; It belongs to the Roman aqueduct to Nîmes and carried it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_de_Born%C3%A8gre]; It crosses a torrent [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_de_Born%C3%A8gre]; The bridge has three arches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_de_Born%C3%A8gre]; Sediment buries two of the three arches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_de_Born%C3%A8gre]; The sediment reaches up to the vaults of the buried arches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_de_Born%C3%A8gre]; Once the aqueduct decayed, people crossed the bridge on foot [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_de_Born%C3%A8gre]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e6e23722-1ab1-48ef-bf6f-252a0135097f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 1st-century AD Roman-Berber city built for army veterans. The name says it plainly — Diana Veteranorum means "Diana''s town for retired soldiers."', 'Settled by veterans of the Legio III Augusta under Trajan, the Ancient City of Diana Veteranorum keeps a paved forum, an aqueduct and a temple perhaps dedicated to the goddess Diana.', 'teaser-card-s031:e6e23722-1ab1-48ef-bf6f-252a0135097f:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9a1d49f1d5c66cad6bd6e38bf5256e9f83aca7ddfd2cfd875cd97bc426575ec0|47428e01284b78e6c853345be20b1ef97da363b10f4b382c67cad2594406feaa|47428e01284b78e6c853345be20b1ef97da363b10f4b382c67cad2594406feaa', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Settled by veterans of the Legio III Augusta under Trajan, the Ancient City of Diana Veteranorum keeps a paved forum, an aqueduct and a temple perhaps dedicated to the goddess Diana."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the city was settled by veterans of the Legio III Augusta [S3]; this happened under Trajan [S3]; the site has a paved forum [S5]; the site has an aqueduct [S5]; the site has a temple [S6]; the temple was perhaps dedicated to the goddess Diana [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-082 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Diana Veteranorum is an ancient Roman city (a settlement/town, not a fort or tomb) in Algeria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Veteranorum]; The city was settled by veterans of the Legio III Augusta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Veteranorum]; The settlement of the veterans happened under the emperor Trajan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Veteranorum]; The site has a paved forum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Veteranorum]; The site has an aqueduct [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Veteranorum]; There is a temple that may (perhaps) have been dedicated to the goddess Diana [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Veteranorum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e732ae7f-cbe5-41a2-a784-90f7731d29f0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Takht-e Soleyman, this Atash Behram — Zoroastrianism''s highest fire grade — served the warrior class. The only one of three supreme fires whose temple has been physically located.', 'Sasanian kings built the Azargoshnasp Fire Temple for one of the three most sacred fires of pre-Islamic Iran. Heraclius sacked it in 623/4, yet the fire burned on into the Islamic era.', 'teaser-card-s031:e732ae7f-cbe5-41a2-a784-90f7731d29f0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '56ff30df0a89a375d7d9871b515c00f75960dd924c1299a9892586f3da53569a|b4b1e5a9018127a3fa43b7cf9ffb3c094d72781ebe95c98fdf867df4e968eaf5|b4b1e5a9018127a3fa43b7cf9ffb3c094d72781ebe95c98fdf867df4e968eaf5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sasanian kings built the Azargoshnasp Fire Temple for one of the three most sacred fires of pre-Islamic Iran. Heraclius sacked it in 623/4, yet the fire burned on into the Islamic era."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sasanian kings built the temple [S3]; the temple was built for a sacred fire that was one of the three most sacred fires of pre-Islamic Iran [S1, S3]; Heraclius sacked the temple in 623/4 [S6]; the fire continued to burn into the Islamic era [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-082 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Azargoshnasp Fire Temple (Adur Gushnasp) is a Zoroastrian fire temple in Iran (at Takht-e Soleyman, West Azerbaijan) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adur_Gushnasp]; Sasanian kings built the temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adur_Gushnasp]; The temple housed one of the three most sacred fires of pre-Islamic Iran [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adur_Gushnasp]; Heraclius sacked the temple in 623/4 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adur_Gushnasp]; The fire survived the sack and continued to burn into the Islamic era [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adur_Gushnasp]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e7e75467-cc43-437e-9221-5ed85c050e63'::uuid, 'card_description', '8th century BC Thracian settlement that became a 35-hectare Late Roman fortress with 32 bastions, a civic basilica, an early Christian basilica with crypts, and a water reservoir.', 'Near today''s Abrit lie the ruins of Zaldapa, a large Late Roman fortified city. In 2015, archaeologists excavating its Christian bishop''s basilica uncovered a stone inscription in Greek.', 'teaser-card-s031:e7e75467-cc43-437e-9221-5ed85c050e63:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '7d4cc48db60422882e0ee3735a8ee9a29ec2d277c4cc98563eaf8a81c9d64da7|18b1fc60473661ff53191c5a5f33f2b7eb170eec9b85f6eed7af6ff2d816a064|18b1fc60473661ff53191c5a5f33f2b7eb170eec9b85f6eed7af6ff2d816a064', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near today''s Abrit lie the ruins of Zaldapa, a large Late Roman fortified city. In 2015, archaeologists excavating its Christian bishop''s basilica uncovered a stone inscription in Greek."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Zaldapa lies near today''s Abrit [S1]; ruins of Zaldapa lie there [S1, S3]; Zaldapa was a large Late Roman fortified city [S1]; in 2015 archaeologists were excavating the ruins of a Christian bishop''s basilica at Zaldapa [S3]; they uncovered a stone inscription in Greek [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-082 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Zaldapa is a city (a settlement, not a single building) of the Late Roman period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]; Zaldapa is fortified [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]; Zaldapa is large (the site covers over 35 hectares) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]; The ruins of Zaldapa lie near today''s Abrit [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]; Zaldapa (and Abrit) is in Bulgaria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]; The site has a Christian basilica that was a bishop''s basilica (Zaldapa was a bishop''s seat) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]; In 2015 archaeologists excavating the ruins of a Christian bishop''s basilica at Zaldapa made a discovery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]; The find was a stone inscription in Greek [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaldapa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e7f9d07d-60a4-4ec3-8ae7-45da301da233'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The acropolis of ancient Argos, fortified from the Bronze Age through the Venetian era. A Byzantine-Venetian castle sits directly above foundations dating to the 1st millennium BC.', 'On a high rocky hill in the west of Argos rises Larisa, its acropolis, in continuous use from prehistoric times until WWII. According to Strabo, it is named for a group of Pelasgians.', 'teaser-card-s031:e7f9d07d-60a4-4ec3-8ae7-45da301da233:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 183 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '041d9abe3d3aca72c9cd46ae4a7c85ad094b376caf07184be2da300d1642199a|9a067ea8a5ce377705101768667bf0b08f0e2560d20d497e75b07a07b078789d|9a067ea8a5ce377705101768667bf0b08f0e2560d20d497e75b07a07b078789d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a high rocky hill in the west of Argos rises Larisa, its acropolis, in continuous use from prehistoric times until WWII. According to Strabo, it is named for a group of Pelasgians."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "on a high rocky hill in the west of Argos [S1]; Larisa is its acropolis [S1]; in continuous use from prehistoric times until WWII [W1]; according to Strabo named for a group of Pelasgians [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify2-006 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Larisa is the acropolis of Argos [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larisa_(Argos)]; It stands on a high rocky hill in the west of Argos [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larisa_(Argos)]; The site was in continuous use from prehistoric times until WWII [SUPPORTED: https://www.argolisculture.gr/en/list-of-monuments/larissa-castle-argos/]; According to Strabo it is named for a group of Pelasgians [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larisa_(Argos)]"}, {"source": "web fact W1", "url": "https://www.argolisculture.gr/en/list-of-monuments/larissa-castle-argos/", "quote": "Although the site was in continuous use from prehistoric times until WWII"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e8ef9219-a255-457a-8f69-f365b1741038'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An open-air museum with reconstructed Bronze Age longhouses where livestock and humans shared a single roof. A burial hut and pit-house complete the village.', 'In 1969, building work at Lake Hitzacker turned up ceramic shards. The Hitzacker Archaeological Centre shows reconstructed Bronze Age longhouses with stalls and living space under one roof.', 'teaser-card-s031:e8ef9219-a255-457a-8f69-f365b1741038:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'e3c789b59aae653d3998c8603bd0aec49151b6a9a0cc53bb66a2c0a290274ab4|ee7fc8835d4dd77dd6ad8b0eacf284c46d47668bc4aec59d4a366b1e6cf329ee|ee7fc8835d4dd77dd6ad8b0eacf284c46d47668bc4aec59d4a366b1e6cf329ee', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1969, building work at Lake Hitzacker turned up ceramic shards. The Hitzacker Archaeological Centre shows reconstructed Bronze Age longhouses with stalls and living space under one roof."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "in 1969 building work at Lake Hitzacker turned up ceramic shards [S5]; the Hitzacker Archaeological Centre shows reconstructed longhouses [S3]; the longhouses are Bronze Age [S2, S3]; the longhouses combine livestock stalls and living space under one roof [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-082 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Hitzacker Archaeological Centre is an archaeological open-air museum in Hitzacker, Germany (Lower Saxony) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitzacker_Archaeological_Centre]; In 1969 building work at Lake Hitzacker turned up ceramic shards [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitzacker_Archaeological_Centre]; The centre shows reconstructed longhouses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitzacker_Archaeological_Centre]; The reconstructed longhouses are Bronze Age (the museum presents Bronze Age settlements) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitzacker_Archaeological_Centre]; The longhouses combine stalls and living space under one roof [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitzacker_Archaeological_Centre]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e9a3f325-9632-4f10-a0a4-b04048f0b117'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Sanctuary at Tegea active from the 10th century BC, dedicated to Athena under a fusion with the local deity Alea. Its status as asylum was so absolute that no one could be seized within its walls.', 'At Tegea, the Temple of Athena Alea honoured a goddess fused from Olympian Athena and the local deity Alea. Endoeus made her cult statue in ivory; Pausanias told of the first temple.', 'teaser-card-s031:e9a3f325-9632-4f10-a0a4-b04048f0b117:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'aa14442be5474e4fd9e03ae9f1298bca0a565f77fd66ed38937d8fb1b79da253|6648786c6fe8cc12721a56e49d05414fad5ff22f8df6f949261735e0896f6d78|6648786c6fe8cc12721a56e49d05414fad5ff22f8df6f949261735e0896f6d78', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Tegea, the Temple of Athena Alea honoured a goddess fused from Olympian Athena and the local deity Alea. Endoeus made her cult statue in ivory; Pausanias told of the first temple."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the temple was at Tegea [S1]; the temple honoured the goddess Athena Alea [S1]; she was a fusion (syncretization) of Olympian Athena and the local deity Alea [S1]; Endoeus made her cult statue [S4]; the cult statue was made in ivory [S4]; Pausanias told of the first temple [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-083 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Temple of Athena Alea is a temple (sanctuary) at Tegea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Alea]; The temple is in Greece (Ancient Greece) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Alea]; It honoured the goddess Athena under the epithet Alea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Alea]; The goddess is a fusion (syncretization) of the Olympian goddess Athena with a local deity [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Alea]; Endoeus made her cult statue [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Alea]; The cult statue was made of ivory [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Alea]; Pausanias told of the first temple at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Athena_Alea]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('e9f5f160-fb1f-4497-b89c-e3e3d4f1e8e7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Late 2nd to early 3rd century AD. This 12-metre sandstone arch guarded Timgad''s western gate — centuries of wagon wheels carved deep ruts into the stone beneath the central arch.', 'Trajan''s Arch was the western gate of Timgad: vehicles left deep ruts under its central arch, while pedestrians took the side arches. Its niches were designed for statues, now lost.', 'teaser-card-s031:e9f5f160-fb1f-4497-b89c-e3e3d4f1e8e7:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '1e947b2f6f26a400c39a3bd4fe2663efdb17da95e4c95a9d90e95b30beca75af|6d167eaeb42fcb00845ab70493c08f7c68f0b01115838fde2fb1c80f31fb2316|6d167eaeb42fcb00845ab70493c08f7c68f0b01115838fde2fb1c80f31fb2316', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Trajan''s Arch was the western gate of Timgad: vehicles left deep ruts under its central arch, while pedestrians took the side arches. Its niches were designed for statues, now lost."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the arch was the western gate of Timgad [S1, S2]; vehicles passed the central arch and left deep ruts under it [S3]; pedestrians used the side arches [S4]; the niches were designed for statues [S6]; the statues are now lost [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-083 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Trajan''s Arch is a Roman arch at Timgad, Algeria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Trajan_(Timgad)]; It was the western gate of Timgad [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Trajan_(Timgad)]; Vehicles left deep ruts under its central arch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Trajan_(Timgad)]; Pedestrians took the side arches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Trajan_(Timgad)]; Its niches were designed for statues, which are now lost [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Trajan_(Timgad)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('eae0879c-2e4a-422d-8626-2fbf21adb227'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The antiquary Erskine Beveridge believed that a second and perhaps a third chamber exist.', 'On North Uist stands Barpa Langass, a Neolithic chambered cairn around 5,000 years old, roofed with massive slabs. Erskine Beveridge believed a second, perhaps a third, chamber exists.', 'teaser-card-s031:eae0879c-2e4a-422d-8626-2fbf21adb227:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '022f8c2847b1a53d7f28d9a25014b9f109b1f98dd8a266aa05e662c981b1616c|a12b5d05baf73ff1a42f96b7b9a8960ba7671fca7c68c9c53a9c157b02ed463f|a12b5d05baf73ff1a42f96b7b9a8960ba7671fca7c68c9c53a9c157b02ed463f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On North Uist stands Barpa Langass, a Neolithic chambered cairn around 5,000 years old, roofed with massive slabs. Erskine Beveridge believed a second, perhaps a third, chamber exists."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Barpa Langass is on North Uist [S1]; it is a Neolithic chambered cairn [S1]; it is around 5,000 years old [S2]; it is roofed with massive slabs [S3]; Erskine Beveridge believed a second, perhaps a third, chamber exists [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-083 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Barpa Langass is a chambered cairn on North Uist [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barpa_Langass]; It is Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barpa_Langass]; It is around 5,000 years old [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barpa_Langass]; It is roofed with massive slabs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barpa_Langass]; Erskine Beveridge believed a second chamber exists [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barpa_Langass]; Beveridge believed perhaps a third chamber exists [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barpa_Langass]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('eb736314-b5da-4853-9859-eb43a30cdcbc'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Two Chalcolithic dolmens from the 4th-3rd millennium BC standing near each other. Paired tombs like this may mark a boundary, a family divide, or successive generations.', 'In a farm field near Évora, the Antas da Valeira, two Chalcolithic dolmens about 100 metres apart, still await study by archaeologists. At one, fallen stones lie between two olive trees.', 'teaser-card-s031:eb736314-b5da-4853-9859-eb43a30cdcbc:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5170747b0c141cb5754a8e902d22a4e19eee388ba19f03134b8c139850b7dd17|2069857290acd64e3d9085d07db64ade04c1a9e6e79898690517a0840f509e6b|2069857290acd64e3d9085d07db64ade04c1a9e6e79898690517a0840f509e6b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In a farm field near Évora, the Antas da Valeira, two Chalcolithic dolmens about 100 metres apart, still await study by archaeologists. At one, fallen stones lie between two olive trees."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Antas da Valeira lie in a farm field [S2]; they are near Évora (in the Évora area) [S2]; they are two Chalcolithic dolmens [S1]; the two are about 100 metres apart [S1]; they have yet to be studied by archaeologists [S3]; at one of them fallen stones lie [S5]; that dolmen lies between two olive trees [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-083 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Antas da Valeira are two dolmens (burial chambers) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]; The dolmens are Chalcolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]; The two dolmens are about 100 metres apart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]; They are near Evora [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]; The dolmens stand in a farm field [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]; They still await study by archaeologists [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]; At one dolmen (Anta da Valeira 1) the site lies between two olive trees [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]; Fallen stones lie at that dolmen [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antas_da_Valeira]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ebd73df9-93c3-4466-8d55-2b7fbceb5df2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 2nd-century AD villa in North Lincolnshire whose 4th-century mosaics are among the most complete from Roman Britain north of the Humber. The 1958–1967 dig produced a benchmark site report.', 'Discovered in 1747, Winterton Roman Villa sets its buildings around a courtyard 300 feet wide, among them three bath-houses. Five large mosaics have been found at the site.', 'teaser-card-s031:ebd73df9-93c3-4466-8d55-2b7fbceb5df2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 172 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '6e111bb28402528754deaeb00711f2091297a54a79a449ad28c3f1cd83b314ad|0638b5654edc5512129a18912c5320a01ebf2df0137ea2c9e86a1ae5aab23944|0638b5654edc5512129a18912c5320a01ebf2df0137ea2c9e86a1ae5aab23944', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Discovered in 1747, Winterton Roman Villa sets its buildings around a courtyard 300 feet wide, among them three bath-houses. Five large mosaics have been found at the site."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the villa was discovered in 1747 [S2]; its buildings surround a courtyard [S6]; the courtyard is 300 feet wide [S6]; the buildings include three bath-houses [S6]; five large mosaics have been found at the site [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-083 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Winterton Roman Villa is a Roman villa at Winterton, North Lincolnshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winterton_Roman_villa]; It was discovered in 1747 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winterton_Roman_villa]; Its buildings surround a courtyard 300 feet wide [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winterton_Roman_villa]; Among the buildings are three bath-houses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winterton_Roman_villa]; Five large mosaics have been found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winterton_Roman_villa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ec4f5013-86b2-45a2-af61-d69b8f863d74'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Lagentium or Legiolum was the Roman name for the fort and surrounding civilian settlement which was built around the year 74 by the Roman Empire.', 'The town of Castleford stands on Lagentium, a Roman fort built around the year 74 on a spot Roman engineers chose for its strongly strategic position. Its name may be of Brittonic origin.', 'teaser-card-s031:ec4f5013-86b2-45a2-af61-d69b8f863d74:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'd626463442834a5b8c790573d62d7deff2ce099c1e7b6bfc2b66986e684c2b81|ba6e2f9984a96dc7834741f4305e0aced587d839990348c8b1b73c0c2d0f512d|ba6e2f9984a96dc7834741f4305e0aced587d839990348c8b1b73c0c2d0f512d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The town of Castleford stands on Lagentium, a Roman fort built around the year 74 on a spot Roman engineers chose for its strongly strategic position. Its name may be of Brittonic origin."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the town of Castleford stands on Lagentium (the former fort) [S2]; Lagentium was a Roman fort [S1]; it was built around the year 74 [S1]; Roman engineers chose the spot for its strongly strategic position [S4]; the name may be of Brittonic origin [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-084 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Lagentium was a Roman fort (with a surrounding civilian settlement), the central claim of the text. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagentium]; The English town of Castleford stands on the site of Lagentium. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagentium]; The fort was built around the year 74. [SUPPORTED: https://lostbritain.org.uk/place/Lagentium/]; Roman engineers chose the spot for its strongly strategic position. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagentium]; The name Lagentium may be of Brittonic origin. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagentium]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ecc0e85d-51c0-47a0-a63f-a427b1f94b01'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman city that became the tribal capital of the Cantiaci and featured a large religious precinct. It eventually grew into modern Canterbury.', 'Where Canterbury stands today, Durovernum Cantiacorum held the best local crossing of the Stour on Watling Street. At its heart rose a forum and basilica, a temple enclosure and a theatre.', 'teaser-card-s031:ecc0e85d-51c0-47a0-a63f-a427b1f94b01:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'cc399a4abcbc3ae6b2261fde4a1d8210476582b1bed3be85c3a2c4411308ce05|816d06b698dab6e5ae57c74a118f13bb53505a7f4b53cefd96ea9bcbe949ce43|816d06b698dab6e5ae57c74a118f13bb53505a7f4b53cefd96ea9bcbe949ce43', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Where Canterbury stands today, Durovernum Cantiacorum held the best local crossing of the Stour on Watling Street. At its heart rose a forum and basilica, a temple enclosure and a theatre."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Durovernum Cantiacorum lay where Canterbury stands today [S1]; it was at the best local crossing of the Stour [S2]; it was on Watling Street [S2]; at its centre was a forum and basilica [S6]; at its centre was a temple enclosure [S6]; at its centre was a theatre [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-084 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Durovernum Cantiacorum was a Roman town at the site of present-day Canterbury (the central claim of the text). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durovernum_Cantiacorum]; It lay on Watling Street. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durovernum_Cantiacorum]; It held the best local crossing of the Stour. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durovernum_Cantiacorum]; At its centre stood a forum and basilica. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durovernum_Cantiacorum]; At its centre stood a temple enclosure. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durovernum_Cantiacorum]; At its centre stood a theatre. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durovernum_Cantiacorum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ee159937-ce30-40a0-b657-48e29c4df37b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A luxurious Roman seaside villa buried by Vesuvius in 79 AD, attributed to Emperor Nero. An inscription mentions Nero''s second wife, Poppaea Sabina.', 'Buried by Vesuvius in 79 AD, about 10 m below modern ground, the luxurious seaside Villa Poppaea hid a large swimming pool, illusionistic frescoes and some 40 marble sculptures.', 'teaser-card-s031:ee159937-ce30-40a0-b657-48e29c4df37b:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 177 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '80883ad03b4722ccf2dbea528bd3f76ed79b321913b000ac8d80d9476a5c2125|a2f53bbc04c2b4dd95a49f6ebb36a7065cbda5bc529f539929814d6165c75e38|a2f53bbc04c2b4dd95a49f6ebb36a7065cbda5bc529f539929814d6165c75e38', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Buried by Vesuvius in 79 AD, about 10 m below modern ground, the luxurious seaside Villa Poppaea hid a large swimming pool, illusionistic frescoes and some 40 marble sculptures."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the villa was buried by Vesuvius in 79 AD [S3]; it lay about 10 m below modern ground level [S3]; it is a luxurious seaside villa [S1]; it had a large swimming pool [S5]; it had illusionistic frescoes [S7]; some 40 marble sculptures were found there [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-084 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Villa Poppaea is a luxurious Roman seaside villa (the central claim of the text). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Poppaea]; It was buried by the eruption of Vesuvius in 79 AD. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Poppaea]; It lies about 10 m below modern ground. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Poppaea]; The villa had a large swimming pool. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Poppaea]; The villa had illusionistic frescoes. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Poppaea]; Some 40 marble sculptures were found at the villa. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Poppaea]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ee2d3797-494f-4588-98e8-23d6d76622c6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Seven hillforts survive in Cheshire, and this promontory fort is among them. Used briefly in the 1st millennium BC, its only recovered artifact is a bronze palstave found in the 19th century.', 'Steep natural slopes and a bank and ditch guard Kelsborrow Castle, an Iron Age hill fort where a bronze palstave was once recovered. Surveys suggest storage pits may lie buried within.', 'teaser-card-s031:ee2d3797-494f-4588-98e8-23d6d76622c6:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '62d43d37259a3a6c500bfb5dad2c99e94e322860c39925c71732e0f51ebdda4c|0c52cf179ade298a0a36fc0c4e75efb03362dcd68c413def82c84ef6c9249839|0c52cf179ade298a0a36fc0c4e75efb03362dcd68c413def82c84ef6c9249839', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-028", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Steep natural slopes and a bank and ditch guard Kelsborrow Castle, an Iron Age hill fort where a bronze palstave was once recovered. Surveys suggest storage pits may lie buried within."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-028 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "steep natural slopes defend the site [S4]; a bank and ditch surround the site [S5]; Kelsborrow Castle is an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; a bronze palstave was recovered from the site [S2]; surveys suggest storage pits may lie buried within the hill fort [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-084 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kelsborrow Castle is an Iron Age hill fort (the central claim of the text). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelsborrow_Castle]; Steep natural slopes help guard the fort. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelsborrow_Castle]; A bank and ditch surround the fort. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelsborrow_Castle]; A bronze palstave was once recovered from the site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelsborrow_Castle]; Surveys suggest storage pits may lie buried within the fort. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelsborrow_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ee80f50f-49c5-49c1-b3a6-76cce8d757a3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Built of Parian marble with rare Aeolian capitals, this Delphi treasury was dedicated by the colony of Massalia — modern Marseille — and stood within the Sanctuary of Athena Pronaea.', 'In the Sanctuary of Athena Pronaia, the Treasury of the Massaliots rose in Parian marble circa 510 BC, two Aeolic columns at its front and a four-horse chariot carved on a pediment.', 'teaser-card-s031:ee80f50f-49c5-49c1-b3a6-76cce8d757a3:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '6bff9cedbe4e4d86fc41a8ce55e02680af2c059600483b5fc45d1ecf7a9636a0|775b391008d4ec6be956e6e6cee0e940e63c96da713ed8c3f5dec534720a3579|775b391008d4ec6be956e6e6cee0e940e63c96da713ed8c3f5dec534720a3579', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Sanctuary of Athena Pronaia, the Treasury of the Massaliots rose in Parian marble circa 510 BC, two Aeolic columns at its front and a four-horse chariot carved on a pediment."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the treasury stood in the Sanctuary of Athena Pronaia [S1, S2]; the building is the Treasury of the Massaliots [S1]; its elevation was built of Parian marble [S5]; it dates to circa 510 BC [S4]; it had two columns at its front, with Aeolic capitals [S3]; a pediment bore a sculpted chariot drawn by four horses [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-084 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Treasury of the Massaliots was a treasury building (a small temple-like structure built by the Massaliots), the central claim of the text. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_the_Massaliots_(Delphi)]; It stood in the Sanctuary of Athena Pronaia (at Delphi). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_the_Massaliots_(Delphi)]; It was built of Parian marble. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_the_Massaliots_(Delphi)]; It dates to circa 510 BC. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_the_Massaliots_(Delphi)]; It had two columns at its front. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_the_Massaliots_(Delphi)]; The columns were Aeolic (had Aeolic capitals). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_the_Massaliots_(Delphi)]; A chariot drawn by four horses was carved on a pediment. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_the_Massaliots_(Delphi)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ef479a70-837a-468b-b107-86751269b431'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Akeman Street linked Watling Street just north of Verulamium with the Fosse Way at Corinium Dobunnorum.', 'A Roman road that may well have been an older track, Akeman Street runs approximately 117 kilometres from Watling Street near Verulamium to the Fosse Way at Corinium, now Cirencester.', 'teaser-card-s031:ef479a70-837a-468b-b107-86751269b431:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 183 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '4e5bcc195578f7b1aeec045b5e00656a1a7f46770ef20bc191e0b468b4553c03|566e8356094a7280bd1d8e7fd6783b516d07615c9ded4cb96232f1249a214d8d|566e8356094a7280bd1d8e7fd6783b516d07615c9ded4cb96232f1249a214d8d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A Roman road that may well have been an older track, Akeman Street runs approximately 117 kilometres from Watling Street near Verulamium to the Fosse Way at Corinium, now Cirencester."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Akeman Street is a Roman road [S1]; the route may well have been an older track [S4]; it runs approximately 117 kilometres [S2]; it runs from Watling Street near Verulamium [S3]; it runs to the Fosse Way at Corinium [S3]; Corinium is now Cirencester [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Akeman Street is a Roman road. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; Akeman Street lies in England (between Hertfordshire and Gloucestershire). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; Akeman Street runs approximately 117 kilometres. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; The road runs from Watling Street. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; The Watling Street end of the road is near Verulamium. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; The road runs to the Fosse Way. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; The Fosse Way end of the road is at Corinium. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; Corinium is now Cirencester. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]; The route may well have been an older track than the Roman road. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akeman_Street]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ef5738eb-4e38-442e-b64e-7a4d8d469f38'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Rock paintings and tombs at 3,160 metres on the slope of Mount Kapuliyuq. Khichuqaqa preserves pigment art alongside ancient burial sites near Urubamba communities in the Andean highlands.', 'High on the slope of the mountain Kapuliyuq, about 3,160 metres up in the Cusco Region, Khichuqaqa is an archaeological site of rock paintings, with tombs nearby.', 'teaser-card-s031:ef5738eb-4e38-442e-b64e-7a4d8d469f38:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 162 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'c7ffc0bca706f6f1d46db1e6921581caa25423709e310c231f14df3bebe077ed|edd18c519077733e89e9425a7d628cef506aa84544039bba582016fb7c12d913|edd18c519077733e89e9425a7d628cef506aa84544039bba582016fb7c12d913', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-rewrite1-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High on the slope of the mountain Kapuliyuq, about 3,160 metres up in the Cusco Region, Khichuqaqa is an archaeological site of rock paintings, with tombs nearby."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check1-002 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies on the slope of the mountain Kapuliyuq [S3]; it lies at a height of about 3,160 metres [S3]; it is in the Cusco Region [S2]; Khichuqaqa is an archaeological site [S1]; it has rock paintings [S1]; there are tombs nearby [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Khichuqaqa is an archaeological site in Peru. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khichuqaqa]; The site has rock paintings. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khichuqaqa]; There are tombs nearby. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khichuqaqa]; The site is in the Cusco Region. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khichuqaqa]; The site lies at about 3,160 metres altitude. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khichuqaqa]; The site is on the slope of the mountain Kapuliyuq. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khichuqaqa]; The site lies high on the slope of Kapuliyuq (the page says only ''on the slope''; no source gives the mountain''s height or the site''s position on it). [UNVERIFIABLE, no proving quote: None]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('efa38e30-8e64-431b-8f87-2b55d8d05968'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An Inca waystation between Runkuraqay pass and Phuyupatamarka on the trail to Machu Picchu. Its name means "inaccessible town" in Quechua.', 'Southwest of the Runkuraqay pass lies Sayacmarca, in the Machupicchu District. Hiram Bingham III visited it in April 1915 and referred to it as Cedrobamba, or cedar plain.', 'teaser-card-s031:efa38e30-8e64-431b-8f87-2b55d8d05968:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 171 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '432cc429f3d81e85144b70926627fd98161fd505511143d0de4d2d33f4182406|9af943854fd96a0b741106bcb032bbde2d086eafe0df7b83d1180500fa22c95f|9af943854fd96a0b741106bcb032bbde2d086eafe0df7b83d1180500fa22c95f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Southwest of the Runkuraqay pass lies Sayacmarca, in the Machupicchu District. Hiram Bingham III visited it in April 1915 and referred to it as Cedrobamba, or cedar plain."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sayacmarca lies southwest of the Runkuraqay pass [S2]; it is in the Machupicchu District [S1]; Hiram Bingham III visited it [S3]; the visit was in April 1915 [S3]; Bingham referred to it as Cedrobamba [S3]; Cedrobamba means cedar plain [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sayacmarca is an archaeological site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Sayacmarca is in Peru. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Sayacmarca is in the Machupicchu District. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Sayacmarca lies southwest of the Runkuraqay site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Sayacmarca lies southwest of the Runkuraqay pass. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Hiram Bingham III visited the site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Bingham''s visit was in April 1915. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Bingham referred to the site as Cedrobamba. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]; Cedrobamba means cedar plain. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayacmarca]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('efaa3f7a-4a10-4d40-84b7-e4a1a49e47c6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Pikillaqta is located 3,200 meters above sea level in the Lucre Basin of the eastern Valley of Cusco.', 'At 3,200 meters in the Lucre Basin lies Pikillaqta, a large Wari site of canals, reservoirs and terraces. All five entrances to the valley from the altiplano were heavily fortified.', 'teaser-card-s031:efaa3f7a-4a10-4d40-84b7-e4a1a49e47c6:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9e3c8f9c8f0b22d690f039061ff62b7e635c08dd9fb74a89430b1961e652e259|97380f0e1103fef0f3e46982f0c2246e6e0601f094930d31243932fee073c447|97380f0e1103fef0f3e46982f0c2246e6e0601f094930d31243932fee073c447', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At 3,200 meters in the Lucre Basin lies Pikillaqta, a large Wari site of canals, reservoirs and terraces. All five entrances to the valley from the altiplano were heavily fortified."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pikillaqta lies at 3,200 meters [S2]; it is in the Lucre Basin [S2]; it is a large Wari site [S1]; it had canals [S5]; it had reservoirs [S5]; it had terraces [S5]; all five entrances to the valley from the altiplano were fortified [S6]; the fortification was heavy [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pikillaqta is an archaeological site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; Pikillaqta is a Wari site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; Pikillaqta is a large site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; Pikillaqta lies at 3,200 metres above sea level. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; Pikillaqta lies in the Lucre Basin. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; The site has canals. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; The site has reservoirs. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; The site has terraces. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; There were five entrances to the valley from the altiplano. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; All of the five entrances to the valley from the altiplano were fortified. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]; The fortification of the entrances was heavy. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f13da15c-a8df-4b8b-9346-4b0c529a3a26'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Neolithic settlement from 3500-3100 BC that overturned views of Stone Age life in the far north. People here were not nomads -- they lived in permanent villages.', 'By the Ii River lies Kierikki, where excavations began around 1960 and are still ongoing. In the summer of 2007 the diggers found Stone Age chewing gum made of birch bark.', 'teaser-card-s031:f13da15c-a8df-4b8b-9346-4b0c529a3a26:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 171 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '7a18748b1409cd72966c1a695a1f71432551f422e24d238ca633f601a43a72b7|adb204804451173d943b2a19830575861383efbf0921dc1eeebb6f12711114f1|adb204804451173d943b2a19830575861383efbf0921dc1eeebb6f12711114f1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "By the Ii River lies Kierikki, where excavations began around 1960 and are still ongoing. In the summer of 2007 the diggers found Stone Age chewing gum made of birch bark."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kierikki lies by the Ii River [S1]; excavations began around 1960 [S4]; excavations are still ongoing [S4]; in the summer of 2007 excavators found chewing gum [S5]; the chewing gum is Stone Age [S5]; the chewing gum is made of birch bark [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kierikki is an area by the Ii River in Finland. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kierikki]; Kierikki is an archaeological excavation site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kierikki]; Excavations at Kierikki began around 1960. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kierikki]; The excavations at Kierikki are still ongoing. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kierikki]; Stone Age chewing gum was found at Kierikki in the summer of 2007. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kierikki]; The Stone Age (Neolithic) chewing gum was made from birch bark tar and was found on a dig at the Kierikki Centre in Finland (reported 20 Aug 2007). [SUPPORTED: https://www.theguardian.com/science/2007/aug/20/archaeology]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f1bf0bf8-26a8-4e50-ab3d-702b208bdad5'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Roman auxiliary fort built in the 70s AD, housing up to 800 infantry across a 2.1-hectare enclosure. Abandoned ~130 AD, it once supported ~2,000 people overlooking a Roman bridge over the river.', 'In 1959, parchmarks seen from the air revealed Trawscoed Fort, above the Roman bridge over the River Ystwyth: barracks for up to 800 infantry, and perhaps around 2,000 people at its height.', 'teaser-card-s031:f1bf0bf8-26a8-4e50-ab3d-702b208bdad5:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '63fad3e8b4f267b9a66324f04b4f6138cbcdb09cd367c21e7746160bcca03e94|aac2fe6c4635dfc313a3a3ecd0230c9d989f286dc6dc26b1fa6cc75bfc743e4b|aac2fe6c4635dfc313a3a3ecd0230c9d989f286dc6dc26b1fa6cc75bfc743e4b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1959, parchmarks seen from the air revealed Trawscoed Fort, above the Roman bridge over the River Ystwyth: barracks for up to 800 infantry, and perhaps around 2,000 people at its height."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site was identified in 1959 [S4]; it was identified through aerial observation of parchmarks [S4]; the fort overlooks the Roman bridge over the River Ystwyth [S3]; it had barracks for up to 800 infantry [S5]; it could have had around 2,000 people at its height (hedged) [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Trawscoed Fort is a fort (an auxiliary Roman fort) in Wales [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trawscoed_fort]; In 1959 parchmarks seen from the air (aerial photography) revealed the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trawscoed_fort]; The fort lies above (overlooks) the Roman bridge over the River Ystwyth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trawscoed_fort]; The fort had barracks for up to 800 infantry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trawscoed_fort]; At its height it perhaps had a total population of around 2,000 people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trawscoed_fort]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f1e8a463-b2c4-42c2-a0b8-94e7bf420f6a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The remains of the Mithraeum in Konjic were discovered in January 1897, at the banks of river Trešanica, under village Repovci, on the outskirts of Konjic.', 'By the river Trešanica stood the Konjic Mithraeum, an early 4th century AD temple to Mithra. Its gray limestone relief shows a Tauroctony on one side and a ritual meal on the other.', 'teaser-card-s031:f1e8a463-b2c4-42c2-a0b8-94e7bf420f6a:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'f58f56a760e487c82aa08bbe7aba44689f27175bc38505c91d947d6d038f4df7|195c0326de5cfe7f17e00f9eaae67ae8b18ab81aa0a2f27aea7b388c957e13a8|195c0326de5cfe7f17e00f9eaae67ae8b18ab81aa0a2f27aea7b388c957e13a8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "By the river Trešanica stood the Konjic Mithraeum, an early 4th century AD temple to Mithra. Its gray limestone relief shows a Tauroctony on one side and a ritual meal on the other."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Mithraeum stood by the river Trešanica [S2]; it is the Konjic Mithraeum [S1]; it was an early 4th century AD temple [S1]; it was dedicated to Mithra [S1]; its relief is carved in gray limestone [S4]; the relief shows a Tauroctony on one side [S4]; the relief shows a ritual meal on the other side [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Konjic Mithraeum is a Mithraeum, a temple (in Konjic, Bosnia and Herzegovina) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjic_Mithraeum]; The temple was dedicated to Mithra [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjic_Mithraeum]; The temple dates to the early 4th century AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjic_Mithraeum]; It stood by (at the banks of) the river Trešanica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjic_Mithraeum]; Its relief is carved in gray limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjic_Mithraeum]; The relief shows a Tauroctony on one side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjic_Mithraeum]; The relief shows a ritual meal on the other side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjic_Mithraeum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f23a32f2-4b00-4b17-9cfd-2f30f753276e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The earthworks of Figsbury Ring are sub-circular and enclose 6.4 hectares of grassland on a chalk ridge in Wiltshire to the north east of Salisbury, in the parish of Firsdown.', 'On a chalk ridge north east of Salisbury, the earthworks of Figsbury Ring enclose 6.4 hectares of grassland. Small portions were dug in 1924, yet it is still to be fully investigated.', 'teaser-card-s031:f23a32f2-4b00-4b17-9cfd-2f30f753276e:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 183 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '3d3845935c7a0319afde8cb2a67666e3d530272211483f4d4381a949cbfbedbc|4825dddedf48c11169811fdba69e1df385e174f3867ebc8d6b4ed148f7a57875|4825dddedf48c11169811fdba69e1df385e174f3867ebc8d6b4ed148f7a57875', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a chalk ridge north east of Salisbury, the earthworks of Figsbury Ring enclose 6.4 hectares of grassland. Small portions were dug in 1924, yet it is still to be fully investigated."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Figsbury Ring lies on a chalk ridge [S2]; it is north east of Salisbury [S2]; its earthworks enclose 6.4 hectares [S2]; the enclosed land is grassland [S2]; small portions were excavated [S4]; the excavation was in 1924 [S4]; the site has yet to be fully investigated [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Figsbury Ring is an earthwork enclosure (hillfort/henge-type site) in Wiltshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Figsbury_Ring]; It stands on a chalk ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Figsbury_Ring]; It lies north east of Salisbury [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Figsbury_Ring]; The earthworks enclose 6.4 hectares [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Figsbury_Ring]; The enclosed area is grassland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Figsbury_Ring]; Small portions of the site were dug (excavated) in 1924 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Figsbury_Ring]; The site is still yet to be fully investigated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Figsbury_Ring]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f2da07c6-4f70-4620-989c-f9f3c5ec07e6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A hilltop site with 6 to 8 meter stone structures surrounded by a 10-meter wall. Petroglyphs of spirals and human faces cover some of the walls.', 'About 3,474 metres up, above the village that shares its name, Urpish is ringed by a wall 8 m to 10 m high, and some of its stony walls bear petroglyphs of spiral figures and human faces.', 'teaser-card-s031:f2da07c6-4f70-4620-989c-f9f3c5ec07e6:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'd4af8963898f7e99bc6c85908dac827715d0698b34abbf0a79981281fa1ac6c2|785ef14ef3973eac4c46d823d51ec53d5980d9704878e57fac1a177e6714e593|785ef14ef3973eac4c46d823d51ec53d5980d9704878e57fac1a177e6714e593', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "About 3,474 metres up, above the village that shares its name, Urpish is ringed by a wall 8 m to 10 m high, and some of its stony walls bear petroglyphs of spiral figures and human faces."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is about 3,474 metres up [S2]; it lies above the village that shares its name [S2]; it is surrounded by a wall 8 m to 10 m high [S3]; some of its stony walls bear petroglyphs [S4]; the petroglyphs show spiral figures [S4]; the petroglyphs show human faces [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Urpish is an archaeological site in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urpish]; It lies about 3,474 metres up [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urpish]; It lies above the village that shares its name (the village of Urpish) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urpish]; The site is ringed (surrounded) by a wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urpish]; The wall is 8 m to 10 m high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urpish]; Some of its stony walls bear petroglyphs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urpish]; The petroglyphs show spiral figures and human faces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urpish]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f2de39f0-bf5a-4da0-8be0-d06bb65f1af2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Home to the largest pyramid by volume in the world, dwarfing even Egypt''s Great Pyramid. The massive structure accumulated over centuries of successive cultures building on top of each other.', 'West of Puebla lies the Zona Arqueológica de Cholula, a key religious center of ancient Mexico. Its immense pyramid measures 4.45 million cubic meters, the largest by volume in the world.', 'teaser-card-s031:f2de39f0-bf5a-4da0-8be0-d06bb65f1af2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5c7025171861bcad38835f08516eceb49b1f6be6761da61b09886e6fd026d178|15300b0f784aabe055a7b745b405869b4b85eb4399499b75eda4cef2c2ed1c33|15300b0f784aabe055a7b745b405869b4b85eb4399499b75eda4cef2c2ed1c33', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "West of Puebla lies the Zona Arqueológica de Cholula, a key religious center of ancient Mexico. Its immense pyramid measures 4.45 million cubic meters, the largest by volume in the world."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies west of Puebla [S2]; Cholula was a key religious center of ancient Mexico [S4]; it has an immense pyramid [S3]; the pyramid measures 4.45 million cubic meters [S3]; it is the largest pyramid by volume in the world [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Cholula archaeological zone (Zona Arqueologica de Cholula) is an archaeological site in Cholula, Puebla, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Pyramid_of_Cholula]; It lies west of Puebla [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholula_(Mesoamerican_site)]; It was a key religious center of ancient Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholula_(Mesoamerican_site)]; Its pyramid has a volume of 4.45 million cubic meters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Pyramid_of_Cholula]; It is the largest pyramid by volume in the world [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Pyramid_of_Cholula]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f4b1324a-06cd-4f3e-a3c7-aeb863180bc0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 6th-9th century AD Maya city with over 200 structures, only a few excavated. It was an independent city before becoming a tributary of nearby Copán.', 'In the Florida Valley, Arqueológico El Puente Park holds more than 200 Maya structures, from tombs to living quarters. Only a few have been excavated, among them a large step pyramid.', 'teaser-card-s031:f4b1324a-06cd-4f3e-a3c7-aeb863180bc0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 183 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '862554752f9b86eb21c4c3dda4b69de5f3d4c266e5a2d544e6188fb523b8129c|8a7a467686cd1f0ff8012b5d903fa64556ad1eea97010ab3061c0c02d094e577|8a7a467686cd1f0ff8012b5d903fa64556ad1eea97010ab3061c0c02d094e577', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Florida Valley, Arqueológico El Puente Park holds more than 200 Maya structures, from tombs to living quarters. Only a few have been excavated, among them a large step pyramid."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in the Florida Valley [S4]; it holds more than 200 structures [S3]; the structures are Maya [S1]; the structures include tombs [S3]; the structures include living quarters [S3]; only a few have been excavated [S3]; the excavated ones include a large step pyramid [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Arqueologico El Puente Park is a Maya archaeological site (a former Maya city) in the department of Copan, Honduras [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Puente_(Maya_site)]; The site lies in the Florida Valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Puente_(Maya_site)]; The site holds more than 200 structures [SUPPORTED: https://sitiomayadecopan.hn/sitios-maya/sitio-arqueologico-el-puente/]; The structures range from tombs to living quarters (tombs are among them) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Puente_(Maya_site)]; The structures include living quarters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Puente_(Maya_site)]; Only a few of the structures have been excavated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Puente_(Maya_site)]; Among the excavated structures is a large step pyramid [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Puente_(Maya_site)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f5e529ea-2e30-4c33-82fc-8ec450f86687'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman military camp on the Pannonian frontier, built in the 2nd century as part of the limes defense system. Abandoned in the 4th century when legions withdrew from the region.', 'The Roman camp of Gerulata was abandoned in the 4th century, when legions withdrew from Pannonia. Its best preserved building is 30 metres long and 30 metres wide, walls 2.4 metres thick.', 'teaser-card-s031:f5e529ea-2e30-4c33-82fc-8ec450f86687:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '3daa9d3fba94f08b60c868918c98396e9c29b896813ad27a434d6401edae8bf3|4c0ed0990ea949ff34325c97e01fbee20007023545189d532a3295b54fed8000|4c0ed0990ea949ff34325c97e01fbee20007023545189d532a3295b54fed8000', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman camp of Gerulata was abandoned in the 4th century, when legions withdrew from Pannonia. Its best preserved building is 30 metres long and 30 metres wide, walls 2.4 metres thick."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Roman camp [S1]; abandoned in the 4th century [S3]; when legions withdrew from Pannonia [S3]; best preserved building 30 metres long and 30 metres wide [S4]; walls 2.4 metres thick [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify2-006 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gerulata was a Roman military camp [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerulata]; The camp was abandoned in the 4th century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerulata]; Legions withdrew from Pannonia at that time [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerulata]; The best preserved building is 30 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerulata]; The best preserved building is 30 metres wide [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerulata]; The walls are 2.4 metres thick [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerulata]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f6825042-70ed-4d70-8e17-42575be53b67'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 40-ton boulder covered in mysterious carved petroglyphs — lines, shapes, and figures whose origin has sparked centuries of debate. No one agrees who made them.', 'Once in the bed of the Taunton River, Dighton Rock is a 40-ton sandstone boulder carved with lines, geometric shapes and drawings of people, of ancient and uncertain origin. Who made them?', 'teaser-card-s031:f6825042-70ed-4d70-8e17-42575be53b67:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '37154cc328574d4ec8d045775b6d5109f7119eaa604b7c2abe9c01624243fce2|5b452d22c8b3527dbd0bf819223f44a9c55c38f3fa54b98da2312f2fef1de84f|5b452d22c8b3527dbd0bf819223f44a9c55c38f3fa54b98da2312f2fef1de84f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Once in the bed of the Taunton River, Dighton Rock is a 40-ton sandstone boulder carved with lines, geometric shapes and drawings of people, of ancient and uncertain origin. Who made them?"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the rock was once in the bed of the Taunton River [S1]; it is a 40-ton boulder [S1]; it is sandstone [S1]; it is carved with lines, geometric shapes and drawings of people [S2, S3]; the carvings are of ancient and uncertain origin [S3]; it is unknown or disputed who made the carvings (implied by the question) [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dighton Rock is a boulder (a rock carved with petroglyphs) at the Taunton River, Massachusetts, USA [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dighton_Rock]; The rock was once in the bed of the Taunton River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dighton_Rock]; The boulder weighs 40 tons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dighton_Rock]; The boulder is sandstone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dighton_Rock]; It is carved with lines, geometric shapes and drawings of people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dighton_Rock]; The carvings are of ancient and uncertain origin [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dighton_Rock]; Who made the carvings is unknown or disputed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dighton_Rock]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f6b8fa8a-775c-4ee3-babd-1723dc03ba86'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The archaeological site Abri de la Madeleine is a rock shelter under an overhanging cliff situated near Tursac.', 'Under an overhanging cliff near Tursac lies the Abri de la Madeleine, type site of the Upper Paleolithic Magdalenian culture. Its finds now rest in museums, the British Museum among them.', 'teaser-card-s031:f6b8fa8a-775c-4ee3-babd-1723dc03ba86:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '75e17975c0826cc17547ec333d99776b753889bc4fee18fdb2631ddad1138db9|c28ad4e50e956d9e87768b4fdc39c8c04188ac8651432ed94e78fac7fcde5d51|c28ad4e50e956d9e87768b4fdc39c8c04188ac8651432ed94e78fac7fcde5d51', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Under an overhanging cliff near Tursac lies the Abri de la Madeleine, type site of the Upper Paleolithic Magdalenian culture. Its finds now rest in museums, the British Museum among them."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies under an overhanging cliff [S1]; it is near Tursac [S1]; it is the type site of the Magdalenian culture [S2]; the Magdalenian culture belongs to the Upper Paleolithic [S2]; its finds are now in museums [S4]; the British Museum holds some of them [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Abri de la Madeleine is a rock shelter (an abri) under an overhanging cliff [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abri_de_la_Madeleine]; The shelter lies near Tursac [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abri_de_la_Madeleine]; It is the type site of the Magdalenian culture [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abri_de_la_Madeleine]; The Magdalenian culture belongs to the Upper Paleolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdalenian]; The Magdalenian culture is named after the Abri de la Madeleine [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdalenian]; Finds from the site are now held in museums [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abri_de_la_Madeleine]; The British Museum holds finds from the Abri de la Madeleine [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/H_Christy-French-Caves-501]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f7145c9f-7760-447f-989c-0f6868405cbb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A multivallate hillfort near Steeple Langford, Wiltshire, occupied from around 300 BC. A 1991 survey found 130 internal structures alongside Iron Age and Roman coins, pottery, and human burials.', 'Three ramparts with outer ditches ring Yarnbury Castle, an Iron Age hillfort in Wiltshire. Around 130 structures hint at long settlement, and an earlier, smaller enclosure lies at its heart.', 'teaser-card-s031:f7145c9f-7760-447f-989c-0f6868405cbb:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9c9f9af69115a89339b9e1ffb96c8720d2fe6c83062ff4eb8e82bab424b6e807|eaf6b3e85fa50b2524890928c3c63737a178d9240ce9d1876c40af52e97cfcef|eaf6b3e85fa50b2524890928c3c63737a178d9240ce9d1876c40af52e97cfcef', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-030", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Three ramparts with outer ditches ring Yarnbury Castle, an Iron Age hillfort in Wiltshire. Around 130 structures hint at long settlement, and an earlier, smaller enclosure lies at its heart."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-030 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "three ramparts with outer ditches [S5]; the ramparts ring (encircle) the site [S5, S7]; Yarnbury Castle is an Iron Age hillfort [S1]; it is in Wiltshire [S1]; there is evidence of around 130 structures [S3]; the structures point to long (prolonged) settlement [S3]; an earlier, smaller enclosure lies at the heart (centre) of the hillfort [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Yarnbury Castle is a hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnbury_Castle]; The hillfort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnbury_Castle]; Yarnbury Castle is in Wiltshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnbury_Castle]; The earthworks consist of three ramparts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnbury_Castle]; The three ramparts have outer ditches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnbury_Castle]; Around 130 structures have been identified within the hillfort [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1005689]; The structures indicate long (prolonged) settlement of the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnbury_Castle]; An earlier, smaller enclosure lies at the heart (centre) of the hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnbury_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f7a0a44f-651b-4dfa-a500-e1a57feb2c2f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A mountaintop leveled and plastered to create a massive plaza, capital of the Zapotec from 500 BC. The ''Danzantes'' — carved stone slabs showing contorted figures — may depict slain war captives.', 'On an artificially leveled ridge rising some 400 m from the valley floor, Monte Alban became the pre-eminent Zapotec center. Its Main Plaza measures approximately 300 meters by 150 meters.', 'teaser-card-s031:f7a0a44f-651b-4dfa-a500-e1a57feb2c2f:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '4c3a6b7e662c9636c36dffac5690e1608d0ec0f6070cbc7bff613f41bfe50ee2|93dca0578ceea2406dca401bd8922f38f513e96bdf8bf7b4e463d2472fb73c91|93dca0578ceea2406dca401bd8922f38f513e96bdf8bf7b4e463d2472fb73c91', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-030", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On an artificially leveled ridge rising some 400 m from the valley floor, Monte Alban became the pre-eminent Zapotec center. Its Main Plaza measures approximately 300 meters by 150 meters."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-030 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monte Alban stands on an artificially leveled ridge [S2]; it rises some 400 m from the valley floor [S3]; Monte Alban became the pre-eminent Zapotec center [S4, S5]; its Main Plaza measures approximately 300 meters by 150 meters [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-088 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monte Alban is a large pre-Columbian settlement/civic-ceremonial center (an ancient Zapotec city) in Mexico (Oaxaca) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Alb%C3%A1n]; The site sits on an artificially leveled ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Alb%C3%A1n]; The ridge rises some 400 m from the valley floor [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Alb%C3%A1n]; Monte Alban became the pre-eminent Zapotec center [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Alb%C3%A1n]; Its Main Plaza measures approximately 300 meters by 150 meters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Alb%C3%A1n]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f7a88213-58c1-4ee4-b791-4babfa0cd67a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Hadrian''s Wall is a former defensive fortification of the Roman province of Britannia, begun in AD 122 in the reign of the Emperor Hadrian.', 'Begun in AD 122, Hadrian''s Wall crossed the whole width of the island, with soldiers in forts, milecastles and turrets. Almost all its standing masonry later went into roads and farmhouses.', 'teaser-card-s031:f7a88213-58c1-4ee4-b791-4babfa0cd67a:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '0c694fb38bdbb31ee3ac3a1d45829fb2e675f13567d0767cc6e416f601a0efcd|f9a60d197d26cd85fcd7c14072dc1976f1d8d55658efa2333f3c6cc45ced8d85|f9a60d197d26cd85fcd7c14072dc1976f1d8d55658efa2333f3c6cc45ced8d85', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-030", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Begun in AD 122, Hadrian''s Wall crossed the whole width of the island, with soldiers in forts, milecastles and turrets. Almost all its standing masonry later went into roads and farmhouses."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-030 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "construction of Hadrian''s Wall began in AD 122 [S1]; the wall crossed the whole width of the island [S2]; soldiers were garrisoned along it in forts [S3]; soldiers were garrisoned in milecastles [S3]; soldiers were garrisoned in turrets [S3]; almost all the wall''s standing masonry was removed later [S4]; the removed masonry was used for roads and farmhouses [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-088 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hadrian''s Wall is a Roman defensive wall (fortification) in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; The wall lies in (northern) England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; It was begun in AD 122 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; It crossed the whole width of the island [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; Soldiers were stationed in forts along the wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; Soldiers were stationed in milecastles along the wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; The wall had turrets in which troops were housed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; Almost all of its standing masonry was later removed and reused [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; The masonry went into (local) roads [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]; The masonry went into farmhouses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fac04898-9342-436f-8a58-df8061d72fcc'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Early Classic Maya site active 250–600 AD in the Petén jungle. Covers 1.3 square kilometres with 729 major structures, near the borders of two neighbouring countries in dense tropical forest.', 'Río Azul, a Maya site of funerary pyramids and ornate tombs, some of them corbel-vaulted, holds an inscription dated to AD 393 that names the Teotihuacan warlord Siyaj K''ak''.', 'teaser-card-s031:fac04898-9342-436f-8a58-df8061d72fcc:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 174 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'b4be7bf76cf493c064a8b3d6913d56bd07b736da9aa894116f2dd6254bf22929|b41251c48a08d5dcf07ea190f4236ca61416ba9a9ef3c898b9f11b82c88b5ff4|b41251c48a08d5dcf07ea190f4236ca61416ba9a9ef3c898b9f11b82c88b5ff4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-030", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Río Azul, a Maya site of funerary pyramids and ornate tombs, some of them corbel-vaulted, holds an inscription dated to AD 393 that names the Teotihuacan warlord Siyaj K''ak''."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-030 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Río Azul is a Maya site [S1]; the site has funerary pyramids [S6]; the site has ornate tombs [S7]; some of the tombs are corbel-vaulted [S7]; an inscription at the site is dated to AD 393 [S5]; the inscription names (mentions) Siyaj K''ak'' [S5]; Siyaj K''ak'' is a Teotihuacan warlord [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-088 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Rio Azul is an archaeological site of the Maya civilization [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]; Rio Azul is in Guatemala [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]; The site has funerary pyramids [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]; The site has ornate tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]; Some of the tombs are corbel-vaulted [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]; An inscription at Rio Azul is dated to AD 393 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]; The AD 393 inscription names Siyaj K''ak'' [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]; Siyaj K''ak'' was a Teotihuacan warlord [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%ADo_Azul]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fbe0c4cf-1863-4930-9bca-1557de689b67'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A cave in a limestone massif with a 372 m route and an entrance at 885 m elevation. It preserves evidence of prehistoric use deep within.', 'Simanya Cave has a 372 m route in the Sant Llorenç del Munt massif. Chalcolithic ceramics were found in 1911; between 1929 and 1930, Pau Codina''s surveys found a large amount of pottery.', 'teaser-card-s031:fbe0c4cf-1863-4930-9bca-1557de689b67:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9fc2baec8bbf272430aa3e654dbfdce941b1719355f34e17129e4c2c2af10072|a7da6dafa748f46308ecfa35477cc82e11087aafbb3f31551905f899a2bbfce3|a7da6dafa748f46308ecfa35477cc82e11087aafbb3f31551905f899a2bbfce3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-rewrite1-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Simanya Cave has a 372 m route in the Sant Llorenç del Munt massif. Chalcolithic ceramics were found in 1911; between 1929 and 1930, Pau Codina''s surveys found a large amount of pottery."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check1-002 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Simanya Cave has a route of 372 m [S2]; it lies in the Sant Llorenç del Munt massif [S1]; Chalcolithic ceramics were found in 1911 [S4]; archaeological surveys took place between 1929 and 1930 [S3]; the surveys were led by Pau Codina [S3]; the surveys found a large amount of pottery [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-088 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Simanya Cave is a cave [SUPPORTED: https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cova_Simanya]; The cave lies in the Sant Llorenç del Munt massif [SUPPORTED: https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cova_Simanya]; The cave has a 372 m route [SUPPORTED: https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cova_Simanya]; Chalcolithic ceramics were found in the cave in 1911 [SUPPORTED: https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cova_Simanya]; Archaeological surveys were carried out in the cave between 1929 and 1930 [SUPPORTED: https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cova_Simanya]; The 1929-1930 surveys were directed by Pau Codina [SUPPORTED: https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cova_Simanya]; Pau Codina''s surveys found a large amount of pottery [SUPPORTED: https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cova_Simanya]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fbe56df2-9a50-4b70-a012-f2e666d2f2f9'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A part of the Classic Veracruz culture, El Tajín flourished from 600 to 1200 AD and during this time numerous temples, palaces, ballcourts, and pyramids were built.', 'In the rolling mountains of Veracruz, El Tajín flourished from 600 to 1200 AD, raising temples, palaces, pyramids and 20 ballcourts. Only about fifty percent of its buildings are excavated.', 'teaser-card-s031:fbe56df2-9a50-4b70-a012-f2e666d2f2f9:card_description', 'teaser-card-s031 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'b7feb9ca08317ca0d1d44daf51e0662448f964a3c4065984fe8b2c9d5c20b1d7|763efe9b3bfac6e9d9aadb6a2a41edfec4c4d9e810c09e5e4a52cff1edbff861|763efe9b3bfac6e9d9aadb6a2a41edfec4c4d9e810c09e5e4a52cff1edbff861', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-write-030", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the rolling mountains of Veracruz, El Tajín flourished from 600 to 1200 AD, raising temples, palaces, pyramids and 20 ballcourts. Only about fifty percent of its buildings are excavated."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-check-030 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "El Tajín lies in the rolling mountains [S5]; it is in Veracruz [S1, S4]; El Tajín flourished from 600 to 1200 AD [S2]; temples were built there [S2]; palaces were built there [S2]; pyramids were built there [S2]; the site has 20 ballcourts [S2, S3]; only about fifty percent of the city''s buildings are excavated [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-088 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "El Tajin is a pre-Columbian city/archaeological site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Taj%C3%ADn]; 